+# 01: Upgrade with a prorated invoice
+
+**What to build:** A customer on an active subscription can upgrade to a plan with a higher monthly list price, without changing interval. The upgrade takes effect immediately and keeps the same billing period. The customer gets an invoice that credits the current plan's unused days and charges the new plan for the same days, so no day is paid for twice. This ticket introduces the plan change operation (`BillingService.change_plan(subscription_id, new_plan_code, new_interval=None) -> Invoice | None`), the `InvalidPlanChange` error, and every validation rule except cancelling a scheduled change. Downgrades, lateral changes and interval switches are rejected with `InvalidPlanChange` until ticket 02 lands, so the service never accepts a change it can't carry out. See the spec (Validation, Classification, Upgrade), the glossary, and ADR 0001.
+
+**Blocked by:** None (can start immediately)
+
+**Status:** resolved
+
+- [ ] `InvalidPlanChange` exists as a subclass of `BillingError`.
+- [ ] `change_plan` with `new_interval=None` targets the subscription's current interval.
+- [ ] Rejections are raised in this order, and each one leaves the subscription and its invoices untouched:
+  - [ ] unknown subscription → `SubscriptionNotFound`
+  - [ ] canceled subscription → `SubscriptionNotActive`
+  - [ ] current period end on or before today (renewal overdue, including trials) → `InvalidPlanChange`
+  - [ ] unknown target plan → `PlanNotFound`
+  - [ ] same plan and same interval → `InvalidPlanChange` (ticket 03 adds the case where a change is scheduled)
+  - [ ] retired target plan → `PlanNotAvailable`, including an interval switch on the retired plan the customer is already on
+- [ ] Moving off a retired plan to an active, more expensive plan is allowed.
+- [ ] Upgrade on an active subscription: the plan changes immediately, and the interval and period dates stay the same.
+- [ ] The invoice is dated today, recorded in the store and returned. It has two lines, both at the current interval's prices:
+  - [ ] unused-time credit: `−(current price × r / d)`
+  - [ ] remaining-time charge: `+(new price × r / d)`
+  - [ ] `r` is the days from today (inclusive) to the period end (exclusive), `d` is the number of days in the current period, and each line is worked out exactly and rounded half-up to cents on its own.
+- [ ] The invoice total is the sum of the two rounded lines. Test: basic → pro for the period Jan 15 to Feb 15, changed on Feb 12, gives −0.97 and +2.90, total 1.93.
+- [ ] An upgrade on the period's first day credits the whole current price and charges the whole new price.
+- [ ] An annual upgrade is prorated at annual prices over the days left in the year.
+- [ ] An upgrade from a free plan still has a `0.00` credit line.
+- [ ] A second upgrade in the same period credits the plan from the first upgrade.
+- [ ] If the summed total is 0 or less (e.g. basic annual → "plus" at 12 a month or 90 a year), the plan still changes, no invoice is recorded, and `None` is returned.
+- [ ] The renewal after an upgrade bills the new plan at full price.
+- [ ] Downgrades, lateral changes and interval switches raise `InvalidPlanChange` for now and change nothing (ticket 02 replaces this).
+- [ ] The README layout line for the service lists the new operation.
+
+## Comments
+
+Resolved on integration branch `plan-changes` (commits 191cb47; review follow-ups in cc06959).
