@@ -0,0 +1,171 @@
+# Plan changes: prorated upgrades, scheduled downgrades and interval switches
+
+Status: resolved
+
+Vocabulary follows `GLOSSARY.md` (Plan change, Upgrade, Downgrade, Lateral change, Interval switch, Scheduled change, Proration, Unused-time credit, Remaining-time charge). Policy recorded in ADR 0001 (plan changes defer downgrades, never refund).
+
+## Problem Statement
+
+A customer who wants a different plan, or wants to move between monthly and annual billing, has no way to do it. Support has to cancel the subscription and subscribe the customer again by hand. That loses the subscription's history. It also either makes the customer pay twice for days they had already paid for, or makes support work out a fair amount themselves, every time.
+
+## Solution
+
+The billing service gets a plan change operation. Which kind of change it is depends on the plans' monthly list prices, whatever interval the customer is on or moving to:
+
+- An **upgrade** (the new plan has a higher monthly list price) takes effect immediately and keeps the same billing period. The customer is billed right away. The invoice credits the unused days of the current plan and charges the new plan for the same days, so no day is paid for twice. The next renewal bills the new plan in full.
+- A **downgrade**, a **lateral change** (same monthly list price) or an **interval switch** becomes a **scheduled change**. Nothing is billed when the customer asks for it. At the next renewal the subscription moves to the new plan and/or interval, and that renewal bills its full price. Unused time is never refunded.
+- An upgrade combined with an interval switch is split. The upgrade happens now, prorated at the current interval's prices, and the interval switch is scheduled for the renewal.
+- During a **trial** nothing has been paid yet, so any plan change takes effect immediately and bills nothing. The trial end date stays the same.
+
+A subscription has at most one scheduled change, and each new request replaces it. Asking for the plan and interval the customer is already on cancels the scheduled change.
+
+## User Stories
+
+1. As a customer, I want to upgrade to a more expensive plan in the middle of a billing period, so that I get the better plan straight away without waiting for my renewal.
+2. As a customer who upgrades, I want to pay only for the new plan's remaining days minus the unused days of my current plan, so that I never pay twice for the same day.
+3. As a customer who upgrades, I want my billing period dates to stay the same, so that my renewal date doesn't move.
+4. As a customer who upgrades, I want my invoice to show the credit for my current plan and the charge for the new plan as separate lines, so that I can see how the amount was worked out.
+5. As a customer who upgrades, I want my next renewal to bill the new plan at its full price, so that billing goes back to normal after the change.
+6. As a customer on an annual plan, I want an upgrade prorated at annual prices over the days left in my year, so that what I already paid for the year counts.
+7. As a customer, I want to be able to upgrade more than once in the same billing period, each time credited for the plan I'm on at that moment, so that a second upgrade is also fair.
+8. As a customer on a free plan, I want to upgrade to a paid plan and be charged only for the remaining days, so that starting to pay mid-period is fair.
+9. As a customer, I want to downgrade to a cheaper plan and keep my current plan until the end of the period I've paid for, so that I get what I paid for.
+10. As a customer who downgrades, I want nothing billed when I ask, and the cheaper plan billed at my next renewal, so that the switch is predictable.
+11. As a customer on monthly billing, I want to switch to annual billing from my next renewal, so that I can get the annual price without losing the rest of my current month.
+12. As a customer on annual billing, I want to switch to monthly billing from my next renewal, so that I don't lose the rest of the year I've paid for.
+13. As a customer, I want to downgrade and switch interval in the same request, so that I can make both changes at once and have both take effect at renewal.
+14. As a customer, I want to upgrade and switch interval in the same request, so that I get the better plan now and the new interval from my next renewal.
+15. As a customer, I want to move to a different plan with the same monthly price, taking effect at my next renewal, so that I can make sideways moves without anything being billed now.
+16. As a customer with a scheduled change, I want to change my mind and stay on my current plan, so that the scheduled change doesn't happen.
+17. As a customer with a scheduled change, I want a new request to replace the scheduled change completely, so that the most recent thing I asked for is what happens.
+18. As a customer with a scheduled downgrade, I want an upgrade to take effect immediately and discard the scheduled downgrade, so that I don't end up downgraded at renewal by mistake.
+19. As a customer who repeats a request that is already scheduled, I want nothing to change and nothing to be billed, so that asking twice is harmless.
+20. As a customer in a free trial, I want to change plan or interval immediately without being billed, so that I can try out the right plan before I start paying.
+21. As a customer in a free trial, I want my trial end date to stay the same when I change plan, so that changing plan doesn't extend or shorten my trial.
+22. As a customer whose trial ends, I want to be billed the full price of whichever plan and interval I'm on at that point, so that my first payment matches what I chose.
+23. As a customer on a retired plan, I want to be able to move to another plan, so that I'm not stuck on an old plan.
+24. As a customer on a retired plan, I want to be told I can't switch interval on that retired plan, because that would amount to buying it again.
+25. As a customer, I want to be told I can't move to a retired plan, so that nobody is newly sold a plan that is no longer on sale.
+26. As a customer whose scheduled plan is retired before my renewal, I want the change to happen anyway, so that a change that was accepted is honoured.
+27. As a support agent, I want to make plan changes through the billing service instead of cancelling and resubscribing, so that customers keep their subscription and history.
+28. As a support agent, I want a request to change to the customer's current plan and interval, with nothing scheduled, to be rejected with a clear error, so that a mistake doesn't go unnoticed.
+29. As a support agent, I want a plan change on a canceled subscription to be rejected, so that canceled customers aren't billed.
+30. As a support agent, I want a plan change on a subscription whose renewal is overdue to be rejected, so that I run the overdue renewal first and the proration starts from the correct period.
+31. As a support agent, I want an unknown subscription or plan to raise the existing not-found errors, so that error handling stays the same as the rest of the service.
+32. As a support agent, I want a rejected request to change nothing (no invoice, subscription untouched), so that a failed attempt never needs cleaning up.
+33. As a support agent, I want to see a subscription's scheduled plan and interval, so that I can tell a customer what will change at their renewal.
+34. As a finance operator, I want every invoice issued for a plan change recorded in the store like every other invoice, so that reports and reconciliation include it.
+35. As a finance operator, I want each proration line rounded to cents on its own, and the invoice total to be the sum of the rounded lines, so that the invoice always adds up exactly.
+36. As a finance operator, I want an upgrade that works out to zero or less to bill nothing and refund nothing, so that the never-refund policy holds even when plans are priced unusually.
+37. As a finance operator, I want renewals to apply a scheduled change before billing, so that the renewal invoice is for the new plan and interval.
+38. As a developer calling the billing service, I want plan changes to return the invoice issued for the change, or nothing when nothing is billed now, so that I can show the customer what they were charged.
+39. As a developer calling the billing service, I want leaving out the interval to mean "keep the current interval", so that a plain plan change doesn't accidentally switch interval.
+40. As a developer, I want a specific error for plan change requests that can't be honoured, alongside the existing billing errors, so that I can handle them separately.
+
+## Implementation Decisions
+
+### Operation
+
+- `BillingService` gets `change_plan(subscription_id, new_plan_code, new_interval=None)`, which returns an `Invoice` or `None`. If `new_interval` is `None`, the target interval is the subscription's current interval. Callers depend on exactly this signature.
+- The errors module gets a new `InvalidPlanChange` exception, a subclass of `BillingError`, for requests that can't be honoured. Existing errors are reused wherever they already fit.
+
+### Validation (in this order; a rejected request leaves the subscription and invoices untouched)
+
+1. Unknown subscription → `SubscriptionNotFound`, raised by the store's lookup.
+2. Canceled subscription → `SubscriptionNotActive`.
+3. Overdue renewal: the current period's end is on or before today, so `renew_due` hasn't billed the next period yet → `InvalidPlanChange`. This applies to trials as well.
+4. Unknown target plan → `PlanNotFound`, raised by the store's lookup.
+5. The target (plan and interval) is the same as the current plan and interval:
+   - with a scheduled change: clear it, bill nothing, return `None`;
+   - with nothing scheduled: raise `InvalidPlanChange`.
+6. Otherwise, a retired target plan → `PlanNotAvailable`. This includes switching interval on the retired plan the customer is already on.
+
+### Trial
+
+A subscription that is trialing takes the target plan and interval immediately. The trial period dates and status don't change, any scheduled change is cleared, no invoice is issued, and the method returns `None`. The renewal at trial end bills the plan and interval the subscription is on by then (existing behaviour).
+
+### Classification (active subscriptions)
+
+Compare the target plan's monthly list price with the current plan's monthly list price. The interval plays no part.
+
+- Higher → **upgrade**.
+- Lower → **downgrade**.
+- Equal, for a different plan → **lateral change**.
+- Same plan, different interval → a pure **interval switch**.
+
+### Upgrade
+
+- The plan changes immediately. The interval and the billing period dates stay as they are.
+- Let `r` be the days from today (inclusive) to the period end (exclusive), and `d` the number of days in the current billing period. Both lines use the prices of the interval the customer is on now:
+  - **Unused-time credit**: `−(current plan's price × r / d)`, worked out exactly and then rounded half-up to cents.
+  - **Remaining-time charge**: `+(new plan's price × r / d)`, worked out exactly and then rounded half-up to cents.
+  - Don't go through a rounded daily rate, and don't round the net difference instead of the two lines.
+- Both lines are always present, including a `0.00` credit when coming from a free plan. Each description names the plan, the interval and the date range the line covers.
+- If the sum of the two rounded lines is greater than 0, one invoice holding both lines is issued, dated today, recorded through the existing invoice-issuing path, and returned.
+- If the sum is 0 or less (only possible when the new plan's monthly price is higher but its price at the current interval isn't), the upgrade still applies, but no invoice is issued and the method returns `None`. The excess credit is dropped.
+- If the request also changes interval, the subscription's scheduled change becomes (new plan, new interval). Otherwise any scheduled change is cleared.
+- Every proration is based on the plan the customer is on at the moment of the request, so a second upgrade in the same period credits the first upgrade's plan.
+
+### Downgrade, lateral change, interval switch
+
+The request becomes the subscription's scheduled change (target plan and target interval), replacing any scheduled change already there. Nothing is billed and the method returns `None`. The current plan, interval and period don't change. Repeating a request that is already scheduled has the same effect: it returns `None` and the stored outcome is unchanged.
+
+### Subscription model
+
+- `Subscription` gets two optional fields, `scheduled_plan_code` and `scheduled_interval`, which default to `None`. Either both are set or neither is; construction raises `ValueError` otherwise, the same way it already rejects bad period dates.
+- A scheduled change always takes effect at the current period's end, so no effective date is stored.
+- Canceling a subscription leaves any scheduled change in place. It is never applied, because canceled subscriptions are never renewed.
+
+### Renewal
+
+- When `renew_due` renews a subscription that has a scheduled change, it first switches the subscription to the scheduled plan and interval and clears the scheduled change. The new period then starts on the old period end, its length follows the new interval, and its invoice is for the new plan's full price at the new interval, dated on the period start.
+- If the scheduled plan has been retired since the request, it is applied anyway.
+- During catch-up of several missed renewals, the scheduled change applies to the first renewal only. Every later renewal uses the plan and interval as changed.
+
+### Docs
+
+The README layout line for the service should list the new operation.
+
+## Testing Decisions
+
+- Tests check external behaviour only, through the service's public operations (`subscribe`, `change_plan`, `renew_due`, `cancel`), using the in-memory store and the fixed clock. They assert on:
+  - what `change_plan` returns, or which error it raises;
+  - the subscription read back from the store (plan, interval, status, period dates, scheduled plan and interval);
+  - the invoices recorded for the subscription, including what a later `renew_due` bills.
+- No new seams: the proration arithmetic is only checked through invoice lines and totals, never through a separately tested helper. The domain-object tests get only the both-or-neither rule for the scheduled fields.
+- Prior art: the existing service tests (fixtures: basic at 10 a month or 100 a year, pro at 30 a month or 300 a year, clock at 2026-01-15, `clock.move_to` to move time forward) and the domain tests. New tests may add plans to the store, e.g. "plus" at 12 a month or 90 a year for an upgrade that nets to zero or less, a "team" plan at 30 a month or 270 a year for a lateral change, and a free plan.
+- Cases to cover (one behaviour per test):
+  - Upgrade, monthly, mid-period: invoice dated today with a credit and a charge line, and the correct total. The period and interval don't change.
+  - Upgrade with separate rounding: basic → pro on a period Jan 15 to Feb 15 (31 days), changing on Feb 12 (3 days left), gives credit −0.97 and charge +2.90, total 1.93. Rounding the net instead would give 1.94, so this test catches that mistake.
+  - Upgrade on the period's first day credits the whole current price and charges the whole new price.
+  - Upgrade at annual prices over the days left in a 365-day period.
+  - The renewal after an upgrade bills the new plan in full.
+  - Two upgrades in one period: the second credits the first upgrade's plan.
+  - Upgrade from a free plan has a `0.00` credit line.
+  - An upgrade that nets to zero or less applies the plan, issues no invoice and returns `None`.
+  - Upgrade combined with an interval switch: prorated at the current interval now, interval switched at renewal with full price.
+  - Downgrade, lateral change, and an interval switch in each direction: returns `None`, no invoice, current plan unchanged, scheduled change set. The renewal moves the subscription and bills the full price, and the new period length follows the new interval.
+  - Downgrade combined with switching to annual is scheduled as one change.
+  - Latest request wins: a second scheduled request replaces the first. With `new_interval=None`, the interval part of an earlier scheduled switch is dropped. An upgrade discards a scheduled downgrade.
+  - Requesting the current plan and interval clears a scheduled change. With nothing scheduled, it raises `InvalidPlanChange`.
+  - Repeating the scheduled request returns `None` and changes nothing.
+  - A scheduled plan retired before renewal is still applied.
+  - Changes during a trial: up, down and interval all apply immediately, with no invoice and the trial end unchanged. Trial end then bills the chosen plan and interval.
+  - Errors: unknown subscription, canceled, overdue renewal, unknown plan, retired target, interval switch on a retired current plan, same plan and interval with nothing scheduled. Each one leaves the subscription and invoices unchanged.
+  - Moving off a retired plan is allowed.
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Refunds, credit notes, negative invoices and account credit balances (see ADR 0001).
+- Collecting payment, payment failures, dunning, tax.
+- Changes that take effect on a date of the customer's choosing, or more than one scheduled change in a row.
+- Seats, quantities, add-ons, coupons and discounts.
+- Changing the price of an existing plan.
+- Reactivating canceled subscriptions, and prorating cancellations.
+- Catching up overdue renewals inside `change_plan` (the caller runs `renew_due` first).
+- Notifying customers about plan changes.
+
+## Further Notes
+
+- `LineItem` already accepts negative amounts and `Invoice.total` sums them, so the unused-time credit line needs no change to the invoice model.
+- An upgrade invoice is dated on the request date, unlike renewal invoices, which are dated on the period start.
