+++ b/docs/adr/0001-plan-changes-defer-downgrades-never-refund.md
@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@
+# Plan changes: upgrades are prorated now, everything else waits for renewal
+
+A plan change is an upgrade when the new plan's monthly list price is higher, whatever interval the customer is on or moving to. Upgrades take effect immediately on the same billing period: the customer is credited the current plan's unused time and charged the new plan's remaining time, both at the current interval's prices. Downgrades, changes between plans with the same monthly price, and interval switches (in either direction) are scheduled for the next renewal, bill nothing when requested, and are billed at full price by that renewal. An upgrade combined with an interval switch is split: the upgrade now, the interval switch at renewal.
+
+We never refund unused time. If an upgrade's credit equals or exceeds its charge (only possible with unusual annual pricing), the upgrade still applies and nothing is billed; the excess credit is dropped.
+
+## Considered Options
+
+- **Prorate every change immediately, issuing credit notes (negative invoices) when the customer is owed money.** Rejected: introduces refunds, and annual → monthly would refund most of a year.
+- **Carry an account credit balance forward to future invoices.** Rejected: a new money-moving concept that every renewal would have to consult.
+- **Classify upgrade vs downgrade by the net prorated amount instead of the monthly list price.** Rejected: the same pair of plans could count as an upgrade on one day and a downgrade on another, which customers and support can't predict.
